5     Problem 3: Momentum Prediction

5.1   The building block: Naive Binomial Model
    One immediate issue in identifying "momentum" in competition is that all variables
we observe are mixed with the momentum effect and the effect from the skill level




                                                                             型
difference of the players. In order to "separate out" the effect of momentum, we first
develop a naive model without considering any momentum. Or, equivalently, a model




                                                              模
under the assumption that the points won are identically distributed random variables.
                                                           [5]
We term this model the Binomial M odel, denoted by BM .
    To develop this model, we collect the world rank of players as additional prior




                                                             学
information ... And convert them into relative rank in 2023 Wimbledon competition.
By "relative", we re-rank the 32 players according to their rankings relative to each




                                                       数
other, resulting an array of integers between 1 and 32. Then BM is simply a function
of (Rank1 , Rank2 ).
    Our Binomial model takes the rank of two players then return the "intrinsic proba-
                                   ：
bility" of winning a certain point, which under the naive assumption, stay constantly




                                                                els
across the whole match.
                                  号
   It’s notable that in tennis competition, strength difference between top rank players




                                                              od
are often more evident than players with lower ranks. For example, we may expect
the difference between rank 1 and rank 2 players larger than the difference between
                          众


rank 31 and rank 32 players. On the contrary, the notion of "expected losing round"
                            [5]
                                                             m
can capture this prior well . Specifically, we define
                 公


                                                H
                                    r1 = 6 − log2 (Rank1 )                                          (6)
                                             AT
 信




     Consequently, we can define BM (Rank1 , Rank2 ) := f1 (r1 , r2 ). Set f2 (x, y) = f1 ( x+y
                                                                                             2
                                                                                                ,
x−y
 2
     ) we have BM = f2 (r1 − r2 , r1 + r2 ) . We provide the following approximation for
f2 :
